**What is a branch**

A branch is a separate line of work inside a git repository.
It lets you work on changes without touching the main (stable) code.
The main branch stays safe while you experiment, add features, or fix bugs in another branch.

**Why teams use feature branches**

Teams use feature branches because they:
  1. Allow multiple people to work at the same time without conflicts
  2. Keep the main branch stable and deployable
  3. Make code reviews easier (changes are isolated)
  4. Reduce risk—if something breaks, only the feature branch is affected
  5. Help track work clearly (one branch = one feature or fix)

**One simple real-world example**

Imagine a team building a food delivery app.
The main branch has the live app code.
One developer creates a branch called feature-payment to add online payments.
Another developer works on feature-tracking to add order tracking.
Each developer works independently.When the payment feature is complete and tested,
it is merged into the main branch. If there’s a problem, the main app is not disturbed.

**In short:**

A branch is like making a copy of a notebook page to try new ideas—
only when it’s correct do you add it back to the main notebook.

